Create your `.proto` file under `src/main/proto/` — Maven and Gradle plugins scan this directory by default. Use `proto3` syntax, which eliminates the `required`/`optional` keywords from proto2 and defaults all fields to their zero values (empty string, 0, false).

Define message types for request and response payloads, assigning a unique field number to each field. Then declare a `service` block with RPC method signatures. Three Java options control the generated package layout — `java_package` sets the package, `java_multiple_files = true` generates each message as its own `.java` file instead of nesting inside an outer class, and `java_outer_classname` names the wrapper class.

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

option java_package = "com.example.grpc";
option java_outer_classname = "GreeterProto";
option java_multiple_files = true;

package greeter;

message HelloRequest {
  string name = 1;         // field number 1 — this is the wire identifier
  int32  age  = 2;         // field number 2 — renaming this field is safe
}

message HelloReply {
  string message = 1;
}

service GreeterService {
  rpc SayHello (HelloRequest) returns (HelloReply);              // unary
  rpc SayHelloToMany (HelloRequest) returns (stream HelloReply); // server streaming
}
```

Field numbers are permanent — once assigned, never reuse a deleted field's number. Reserve retired numbers with `reserved 3, 4;` to prevent accidental reuse that would silently misinterpret old data.

After `mvn compile`, the generated stubs appear under `target/generated-sources/protobuf`. The service stub provides an abstract `GreeterServiceImplBase` class — extend it and override each RPC method. Responses flow through gRPC's `StreamObserver<T>` pattern: call `onNext()` to send data, `onCompleted()` to close the stream normally, or `onError()` with a `StatusRuntimeException` for failures.

```java
import com.example.grpc.GreeterServiceGrpc;
import com.example.grpc.HelloReply;
import com.example.grpc.HelloRequest;
import io.grpc.stub.StreamObserver;

public class GreeterServiceImpl
    extends GreeterServiceGrpc.GreeterServiceImplBase {

  @Override
  public void sayHello(
      HelloRequest request,
      StreamObserver<HelloReply> responseObserver) {

    HelloReply reply = HelloReply.newBuilder()
        .setMessage("Hello, " + request.getName())
        .build();                          // immutable once built — thread-safe

    responseObserver.onNext(reply);        // send the response
    responseObserver.onCompleted();        // close the stream
  }
}
```

The builder pattern on message classes is immutable by design — once `build()` is called, the object cannot change. This means generated messages are inherently thread-safe and can be cached or shared across handlers without synchronization. For server streaming, call `onNext()` multiple times before `onCompleted()`. For error cases, call `onError()` with the appropriate `Status` code instead — never call both `onCompleted()` and `onError()` on the same observer.

The rule is simple: **CSRF protection is required when the browser sends credentials automatically.** Cookies are the primary case — the browser attaches them to every request matching the cookie's domain, without any action from the page. A malicious site can make a form POST to `bank.com/transfer` and the browser automatically includes the session cookie. Without CSRF protection, the bank processes it as legitimate.

JWT tokens in the `Authorization` header break this attack vector — the header is never added automatically, so cross-site requests arrive without credentials and get rejected by authentication. This is why stateless JWT APIs disable CSRF by default.

**The modern alternative to CSRF tokens is the `SameSite` cookie attribute.** Setting `SameSite=Lax` tells the browser not to send the cookie on cross-site sub-resource requests (AJAX, form POSTs from other domains), but to allow it on top-level navigation (clicking a link). `SameSite=Strict` blocks even navigation requests. Either setting prevents the CSRF attack without any server-side token validation:

```java
@Bean
public CookieSerializer cookieSerializer() {
    DefaultCookieSerializer serializer = new DefaultCookieSerializer();
    serializer.setSameSite("Lax");   // Prevents CSRF on AJAX/forms
    serializer.setUseSecureCookie(true); // HTTPS only — never omit this
    serializer.setHttpOnly(true);    // No JS access — blocks XSS session theft
    return serializer;
}
```

For **hybrid applications** — a Spring Boot backend that serves both a REST API (consumed by a React SPA) and server-side rendered pages — you often need CSRF for the form-based flows but want to disable it for the API endpoints. Spring Security supports this with separate security filter chains. Apply `csrf().disable()` to the `/api/**` chain and keep CSRF enabled on the web chain. The SameSite cookie approach eliminates this complexity by making CSRF protection a browser-level concern rather than a server-side token dance.

The configuration class defines the entire messaging topology: which destinations the broker manages, which prefix routes to your controller code, and where clients connect. Getting these prefixes wrong produces silent failures — messages disappear without errors.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSocketMessageBroker
public class WebSocketConfig implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer {

    @Override
    public void configureMessageBroker(MessageBrokerRegistry registry) {
        registry.enableSimpleBroker("/topic", "/queue");
        registry.setApplicationDestinationPrefixes("/app");
    }

    @Override
    public void registerStompEndpoints(StompEndpointRegistry registry) {
        registry.addEndpoint("/ws")
                .setAllowedOriginPatterns("*")
                .withSockJS();
    }
}
```

The `enableSimpleBroker("/topic", "/queue")` creates an in-memory broker that handles subscriptions and fan-out for these prefixes. The `/app` prefix tells Spring to route messages to `@MessageMapping` methods. When a client sends to `/app/chat.send`, Spring strips the `/app` prefix and matches `/chat.send` against your controller annotations. The response, annotated with `@SendTo("/topic/public")`, goes to the broker which distributes it to every subscribed client.

The `.withSockJS()` call registers fallback transport endpoints (`/ws/info`, XHR streaming, XHR polling) for clients behind proxies that block WebSocket upgrades. Omitting it means those clients silently fail to connect — no error, just a hanging request.

`@MessageMapping` methods work like `@RequestMapping` in REST controllers, but they process STOMP frames instead of HTTP requests. The return value is automatically sent to the destination specified by `@SendTo`.

```java
@Controller
public class ChatController {

    @MessageMapping("/chat.send")
    @SendTo("/topic/public")
    public ChatMessage sendMessage(@Payload ChatMessage message) {
        return message;
    }
}
```

When a client sends a STOMP frame to `/app/chat.send`, Spring deserializes the payload using Jackson, invokes `sendMessage`, and broadcasts the return value to every subscriber on `/topic/public`. The production mistake to watch for: using `@SendTo("/topic/...")` for user-specific responses. If a private message handler returns with `@SendTo("/topic/messages")`, every connected client receives every private message. Use `@SendToUser` instead, which targets only the session that sent the request.

For server-initiated pushes from background processes, scheduled jobs, or event listeners, inject `SimpMessagingTemplate` and call `convertAndSend()` or `convertAndSendToUser()` from any Spring bean.

```java
@Service
public class NotificationService {

    private final SimpMessagingTemplate template;

    public void notifyUser(String userId, Object payload) {
        template.convertAndSendToUser(
            userId, "/queue/notifications", payload);
    }
}
```

The critical caveat: `convertAndSendToUser` only works if the STOMP session has a `Principal` bound to it. Without a `Principal`, user destinations silently drop messages with no error log — one of the hardest Spring WebSocket bugs to diagnose.

The relay configuration replaces `enableSimpleBroker()` with a single method call that sets up the TCP connection, credentials, and heartbeat intervals.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSocketMessageBroker
public class WebSocketConfig implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer {

    @Override
    public void configureMessageBroker(MessageBrokerRegistry registry) {
        registry.enableStompBrokerRelay("/topic", "/queue")
                .setRelayHost("rabbitmq-host")
                .setRelayPort(61613)
                .setClientLogin("app_user")
                .setClientPasscode("app_pass")
                .setSystemLogin("system_user")
                .setSystemPasscode("system_pass")
                .setSystemHeartbeatSendInterval(10000)
                .setSystemHeartbeatReceiveInterval(10000);

        registry.setApplicationDestinationPrefixes("/app");
        registry.setUserDestinationPrefix("/user");
    }
}
```

The **system heartbeats** at 10-second intervals serve two purposes. They keep the relay's TCP connection alive through NAT gateways and load balancers that would otherwise kill an idle connection after 60 seconds. They also serve as a health check — if three consecutive heartbeats are missed, Spring fires a `BrokerAvailabilityEvent(available=false)`, which is your signal that messages are being dropped. The default heartbeat interval is **0 (disabled)**, meaning in production without this configuration, a dead broker connection can go undetected for minutes while messages silently disappear into the void.

When the relay connection drops — broker restart, network partition, credential rotation — Spring fires `BrokerAvailabilityEvent`. Without listening for this event, your application continues accepting WebSocket connections and routing messages to a dead relay, silently discarding everything.

```java
@Component
public class BrokerHealthMonitor {

    private final AtomicBoolean available = new AtomicBoolean(true);

    @EventListener
    public void onBrokerAvailability(BrokerAvailabilityEvent event) {
        available.set(event.isBrokerAvailable());
        if (!event.isBrokerAvailable()) {
            log.error("STOMP broker relay lost — messages will be dropped");
        }
    }

    public boolean isBrokerHealthy() {
        return available.get();
    }
}
```

When the relay reconnects after a broker restart, Spring re-establishes the system connection automatically, but **client subscriptions are lost**. Clients that were subscribed to `/topic/updates` before the restart are still connected via WebSocket but no longer subscribed on the broker side. They stop receiving messages with no error notification. The fix is client-side reconnection logic that re-subscribes to all active topics when the STOMP connection is re-established.

Expose `available` as a Micrometer gauge and page your team when it stays `false` for more than 30 seconds — long enough to confirm it's not a transient blip. For **RabbitMQ virtual host isolation**, configure `setVirtualHost("/websocket")` on the relay to keep WebSocket traffic separated from your async messaging queues, preventing a subscription storm from starving order processing consumers.

The `HandshakeInterceptor.beforeHandshake()` method runs during the HTTP upgrade request, before the server sends the 101 response. This is your only opportunity to inspect cookies, query parameters, and HTTP headers — all of which disappear after the upgrade completes.

```java
@Component
public class JwtHandshakeInterceptor implements HandshakeInterceptor {

    private final JwtTokenProvider jwtProvider;

    @Override
    public boolean beforeHandshake(ServerHttpRequest request,
                                   ServerHttpResponse response,
                                   WebSocketHandler wsHandler,
                                   Map<String, Object> attributes) {
        String token = extractToken(request.getURI().getQuery());
        if (token == null || !jwtProvider.validate(token)) {
            response.setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
            return false;
        }
        attributes.put("userId", jwtProvider.getUserId(token));
        attributes.put("roles", jwtProvider.getRoles(token));
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public void afterHandshake(ServerHttpRequest req, ServerHttpResponse res,
                               WebSocketHandler handler, Exception ex) {}
}
```

Returning `false` rejects the upgrade and sends the 401 status before any WebSocket resources are allocated. The `attributes` map persists for the lifetime of the WebSocket session and is accessible from STOMP handlers and channel interceptors. This is how identity flows from the HTTP layer into the WebSocket layer — through session attributes, not through headers that no longer exist.

The token delivery question matters: query parameters (`?token=eyJ...`) work universally but appear in server access logs and browser history. HTTP-only cookies avoid this exposure but complicate cross-origin deployments. Most teams use short-lived query parameter tokens (30-second expiry) that are exchanged for a full session identity during the handshake.

After the WebSocket upgrade succeeds, the first STOMP frame from the client is a `CONNECT` command. A `ChannelInterceptor` on the inbound message channel intercepts this frame, reads the identity stored during the handshake, and binds a `Principal` to the STOMP session.

```java
@Override
public void configureClientInboundChannel(ChannelRegistration registration) {
    registration.interceptors(new ChannelInterceptor() {
        @Override
        public Message<?> preSend(Message<?> message, MessageChannel channel) {
            StompHeaderAccessor accessor =
                MessageHeaderAccessor.getAccessor(message, StompHeaderAccessor.class);

            if (StompCommand.CONNECT.equals(accessor.getCommand())) {
                String userId = (String) accessor
                    .getSessionAttributes().get("userId");
                accessor.setUser(() -> userId);
            }
            return message;
        }
    });
}
```

The `accessor.setUser()` call is what makes the rest of the security model work. Without it, `@MessageMapping` methods receive `null` for the `Principal` parameter, `@SendToUser` has no user to target, and `convertAndSendToUser()` silently drops messages. Every downstream component in the STOMP pipeline depends on this principal binding — it's the single point where HTTP identity crosses into the WebSocket world.

Authentication confirms who the user is. Authorization determines what they can do. The `ChannelInterceptor` handles both by inspecting STOMP commands beyond just `CONNECT`. Without destination-level authorization, any authenticated user can subscribe to `/user/admin/queue/reports` and receive another user's private messages.

```java
if (StompCommand.SUBSCRIBE.equals(accessor.getCommand())) {
    String dest = accessor.getDestination();
    String userId = accessor.getUser().getName();
    if (dest.startsWith("/user/") && !dest.contains(userId)) {
        throw new MessagingException(
            "Cannot subscribe to another user's queue");
    }
}
```

This interceptor pattern-matches destinations against the authenticated user's identity and rejects unauthorized subscriptions before any message reaches the broker. Extend it with role-based checks — `/topic/admin.*` destinations require `ROLE_ADMIN`, public topics allow any authenticated user.

For long-lived connections, **token expiration** creates a subtle vulnerability. A JWT issued at connect time might be valid for 24 hours while the WebSocket stays open for days. The connection runs with stale authorization, and the server never notices. Periodic re-validation — either through forced reconnection when the token approaches expiry or through a custom STOMP heartbeat that re-validates credentials — prevents stale sessions from running indefinitely. My approach: store the token expiry timestamp in session attributes and check it on every `SEND` command. When the token is within 5 minutes of expiry, send a STOMP `ERROR` frame prompting the client to reconnect with a fresh token.

Redis pub/sub is the most common choice for WebSocket cross-node messaging because it's lightweight, fast, and most teams already run Redis for caching. Each server instance subscribes to Redis channels that map to STOMP destinations. When any instance publishes a message, Redis fans it out to all subscribers — which includes every server instance.

```java
@Service
public class RedisWebSocketBridge {

    private final SimpMessagingTemplate messagingTemplate;
    private final RedisTemplate<String, String> redisTemplate;

    public void broadcast(String topic, String payload) {
        redisTemplate.convertAndSend("ws:" + topic, payload);
    }

    public void onRedisMessage(String message, String channel) {
        String destination = channel.replace("ws:", "/topic/");
        messagingTemplate.convertAndSend(destination, message);
    }
}
```

The critical design decision is **what goes through Redis and what stays local**. Broadcasting to `/topic/public` must go through Redis because subscribers exist on every node. But a response to a specific client's request can stay local if the requesting client is on this instance. Publishing everything through Redis wastes bandwidth and adds 1–2ms of latency to deliveries that don't need cross-node coordination.

Redis pub/sub has one significant limitation: **no persistence**. If instance 2 is temporarily disconnected from Redis when a message is published, that message is gone — Redis doesn't buffer it for reconnection. For durable message delivery, a STOMP broker relay with RabbitMQ is the better fit.

Even with a shared pub/sub backbone, the load balancer must route all frames for a given WebSocket connection to the **same server instance**. The HTTP upgrade request and subsequent WebSocket frames must land on the same node — otherwise the upgrade completes on instance 1 but frames arrive at instance 2, which has no knowledge of that session.

```nginx
upstream websocket_backend {
    ip_hash;
    server app-node1:8080;
    server app-node2:8080;
}

location /ws {
    proxy_pass http://websocket_backend;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
    proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    proxy_read_timeout 3600s;
}
```

**IP hash** is the simplest sticky session strategy but breaks when multiple users share a corporate NAT gateway — all their connections land on the same instance, creating hot spots. **Cookie-based affinity** distributes more evenly but requires the initial HTTP response to set the cookie before the WebSocket upgrade.

The overlooked configuration is `proxy_read_timeout`. Nginx defaults to **60 seconds** — after 60 seconds of no data, it kills the connection. WebSocket connections are often idle for minutes between messages. Set this to at least 3600 seconds and rely on application-level heartbeats to detect genuinely dead connections.

**Connection draining** during deployments is equally important. Rolling out a new version without draining causes abrupt disconnections across all clients on that instance. They all reconnect simultaneously, potentially overwhelming remaining instances with a thundering herd of upgrade requests. Send a STOMP `ERROR` frame with a reconnection hint before shutting down, giving clients time to reconnect gracefully to healthy instances.

**Choose SSE** for any unidirectional push scenario: notification feeds, live score updates, dashboard refreshes, log tailing, CI/CD build status. The Spring implementation is straightforward — return an `SseEmitter` from a controller method or use WebFlux's `Flux<ServerSentEvent>`.

```java
@GetMapping("/stream/updates")
public SseEmitter streamUpdates() {
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter(TimeUnit.HOURS.toMillis(1));
    executorService.execute(() -> {
        eventSource.subscribe(event -> {
            emitter.send(SseEmitter.event()
                .id(event.getId())
                .name(event.getType())
                .data(event.getPayload()));
        });
    });
    return emitter;
}
```

The `id` field enables resume — when the client reconnects, it sends `Last-Event-ID` in the request header, and your server can replay missed events from that point. The `SseEmitter` timeout (1 hour here) controls maximum connection duration. Set it too low and clients reconnect constantly, creating churn. Set it too high and failed connections hold resources.

One HTTP/1.1 limitation to account for: browsers cap SSE at **6 connections per origin**. Open 7 dashboard tabs and the 7th tab blocks until one closes. Under HTTP/2, this disappears because SSE streams multiplex over a single connection. WebSocket connections don't have this browser-imposed limit under either HTTP version.

**Choose WebSocket** when the client must continuously stream data upstream through the same persistent connection — chat message input, collaborative document editing where every keystroke is transmitted, multiplayer game state at 30–60 frames per second. If upstream data is infrequent (occasional button clicks, form submissions), prefer REST POST requests alongside an SSE push channel. The **REST+SSE hybrid** gives you real-time push with the simplicity of standard HTTP mutations — and avoids dedicated sticky sessions, custom monitoring, and reconnection logic that WebSocket demands.

The test class must start a real embedded server because the broker, channel pipeline, and SockJS transports only activate with an actual network listener. `RANDOM_PORT` avoids port conflicts when tests run in parallel on CI.

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class WebSocketIntegrationTest {

    @LocalServerPort
    private int port;

    private WebSocketStompClient stompClient;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        List<Transport> transports = List.of(
            new WebSocketTransport(new StandardWebSocketClient()));
        stompClient = new WebSocketStompClient(new SockJsClient(transports));
        stompClient.setMessageConverter(new MappingJackson2MessageConverter());
    }
}
```

Using `SockJsClient` instead of a raw `StandardWebSocketClient` is deliberate — it tests the same transport negotiation that production clients use, catching SockJS configuration errors (missing `/info` endpoint, CORS issues) that a raw WebSocket client would bypass. The `MappingJackson2MessageConverter` ensures the test exercises the same JSON serialization path as production. If your server uses a custom message converter, the test must match or it will succeed with different serialization behavior than production — a silent correctness bug.

The core pattern uses a `BlockingQueue` to bridge the asynchronous frame handler callback with the synchronous test assertion. The `StompFrameHandler` receives messages on an IO thread and offers them to the queue; the test polls the queue with a timeout.

```java
@Test
void messageSentToChatReachesTopicSubscribers() throws Exception {
    BlockingQueue<ChatMessage> received = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();

    StompSession session = stompClient
        .connectAsync("ws://localhost:" + port + "/ws",
            new StompSessionHandlerAdapter() {})
        .get(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

    session.subscribe("/topic/public", new StompFrameHandler() {
        @Override
        public Type getPayloadType(StompHeaders headers) {
            return ChatMessage.class;
        }

        @Override
        public void handleFrame(StompHeaders headers, Object payload) {
            received.offer((ChatMessage) payload);
        }
    });

    session.send("/app/chat.send", new ChatMessage("Alice", "Hello!"));

    ChatMessage result = received.poll(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    assertNotNull(result, "Should receive broadcast within 3 seconds");
    assertEquals("Alice", result.getSender());
    session.disconnect();
}
```

The 3-second timeout in `poll()` is the critical design choice. Too short (500ms) and tests flake on slow CI machines. Too long (30 seconds) and a genuine failure wastes developer time waiting. Three seconds gives enough margin for broker routing, serialization, and network round trips while still failing fast when something is broken. Never use `Thread.sleep` — it either wastes time when things work or still isn't long enough when CI is slow.

The most important test for WebSocket security isn't the happy path — it's verifying that unauthorized connections are actually rejected. If your `HandshakeInterceptor` has a bug that allows anonymous connections, only a test that attempts an unauthorized connection will catch it.

```java
@Test
void connectionWithInvalidTokenIsRejected() {
    String url = "ws://localhost:" + port + "/ws?token=invalid";
    assertThrows(ExecutionException.class, () ->
        stompClient.connectAsync(url, new StompSessionHandlerAdapter() {})
                   .get(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
    );
}

@Test
void userSpecificMessagesRouteCorrectly() throws Exception {
    BlockingQueue<Notification> queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();

    StompHeaders headers = new StompHeaders();
    headers.add("token", "valid-jwt-for-alice");

    StompSession session = stompClient
        .connectAsync("ws://localhost:" + port + "/ws",
            new WebSocketHttpHeaders(), headers,
            new StompSessionHandlerAdapter() {})
        .get(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

    session.subscribe("/user/queue/notifications",
        new StompFrameHandler() {
            @Override
            public Type getPayloadType(StompHeaders h) {
                return Notification.class;
            }

            @Override
            public void handleFrame(StompHeaders h, Object payload) {
                queue.offer((Notification) payload);
            }
        });

    // Trigger server-side push via REST or direct template call
    Notification result = queue.poll(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    assertNotNull(result);
    session.disconnect();
}
```

The user destination test is critical because `/user/queue/notifications` involves destination resolution through `UserDestinationResolver`, which silently drops messages if the principal isn't bound correctly — no error, no log, just missing messages. A passing test confirms the entire chain: handshake authentication, principal binding in the `ChannelInterceptor`, user destination resolution, and message delivery. My testing rule: every WebSocket endpoint gets three test types — a broadcast flow test, a security rejection test, and a user-destination test. These catch the three most common production failures.

STOMP heartbeats are negotiated during the `CONNECT`/`CONNECTED` handshake. Both client and server advertise their heartbeat capabilities as a pair of millisecond values: the minimum interval between sent heartbeats and the minimum acceptable interval between received heartbeats.

```java
@Override
public void configureMessageBroker(MessageBrokerRegistry registry) {
    registry.enableSimpleBroker("/topic", "/queue")
            .setHeartbeatValue(new long[]{10000, 10000});
}
```

The `setHeartbeatValue(new long[]{10000, 10000})` means the server will send a heartbeat at least every 10 seconds and expects the client to send one at least every 10 seconds. A STOMP heartbeat is a single line-feed character (`\n`) on the wire — virtually zero bandwidth. If the server doesn't receive a client heartbeat within the expected interval, it declares the session dead.

The common misconfiguration is setting these values to **zero**, which disables heartbeats entirely. The connection appears stable during development because the network path is localhost — no NAT, no proxy, no mobile handoff. In production, the same zero-heartbeat configuration means dead connections aren't detected until the TCP keepalive timer fires, which defaults to **2 hours on Linux**. Each ghost session holds subscriptions, memory, and a slot in your session registry.

When a connection terminates — whether from a clean close frame, a heartbeat timeout, or a transport error — Spring fires `SessionDisconnectEvent`. This event is your hook for cleaning up user-specific resources: removing from presence maps, flushing pending message buffers, updating status indicators, and releasing locks.

```java
@Component
public class SessionLifecycleHandler {

    private final ConcurrentMap<String, UserSession> sessions =
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    @EventListener
    public void onConnect(SessionConnectedEvent event) {
        StompHeaderAccessor accessor =
            StompHeaderAccessor.wrap(event.getMessage());
        sessions.put(accessor.getSessionId(),
            new UserSession(event.getUser(), Instant.now()));
    }

    @EventListener
    public void onDisconnect(SessionDisconnectEvent event) {
        UserSession removed = sessions.remove(event.getSessionId());
        if (removed != null) {
            broadcastPresenceUpdate(removed.getUser(), "offline");
        }
    }
}
```

The `SessionDisconnectEvent` carries a `CloseStatus` that reveals why the connection ended. **1000** is a normal close (user navigated away). **1001** means the endpoint is going away (server shutting down). **1006** means abnormal closure — no close frame was received, which indicates a network drop. Monitoring the distribution of close status codes in production is a powerful diagnostic tool. A spike in 1006 codes correlates with infrastructure problems: NAT gateway flapping, load balancer idle timeout misconfiguration, or network instability.

For production monitoring, expose the active session count as a Micrometer gauge and set alerts on two conditions: total count exceeding your per-instance memory budget, and a sudden drop indicating a mass disconnection event. Track the ratio of connect events to disconnect events over a rolling window — a growing gap means sessions are leaking.
